---
// Intercom Messenger. Rendered once from BaseLayout, only when the caller has already
// decided this is a real production build — see BaseLayout for the
// PUBLIC_INTERCOM_APP_ID / build-context gating.
//
// `surface` is stamped on the visitor as the `ie_source` custom attribute. Intercom's
// "Stamp conversation Source" workflow reads it when a conversation starts and writes
// the `Source` conversation attribute (Website / Help Center / Platform), which is what
// the source-specific Inbox views filter on. The strings below must match that
// workflow's conditions exactly — change one and you must change the other.
//
// Two deliberate deviations from Intercom's stock snippet, both required here:
//
// 1. No *.netlify.app bail-out, unlike src/components/seo/Analytics.astro. Pre-launch
//    the marketing site's ONLY host IS innerexplorerwebsitev2.netlify.app, so a host
//    guard would mean the Messenger never loads on it. Deploy previews and branch
//    deploys are already excluded by the build-context gate in BaseLayout, so this
//    still can't generate conversations from throwaway URLs.
//
// 2. The whole loader re-runs on `astro:after-swap` (see initMessenger below), because
//    ClientRouter replaces <body> on every client-side navigation and that discards the
//    nodes Intercom appends there. Verified against this workspace, in the order a
//    reader would guess:
//      - `Intercom('update')` / `Intercom('boot')`: no-op. The loaded widget still
//        believes it is booted, so it never rebuilds its DOM — the launcher stays gone.
//      - `Intercom('shutdown')` then `boot`: also no-op, immediately or on a later tick.
//      - Moving Intercom's nodes into event.newDocument in `astro:before-swap`: the
//        launcher reappears but is DEAD — the move adopts #intercom-frame across
//        documents, which reloads the iframe and breaks the messaging channel.
//    Re-executing widget.js is what actually works. It is a memory-cache hit, and the
//    visitor is keyed by the intercom-id-<app_id> cookie, which this path leaves intact
//    (confirmed: same visitor id before and after), so re-running it does NOT create a
//    fresh anonymous visitor the way `shutdown` would.
interface Props {
  appId: string;
  surface: 'Website' | 'Help Center';
}
const { appId, surface } = Astro.props;
---

<script define:vars={{ appId, surface }}>
  window.intercomSettings = { app_id: appId, ie_source: surface };

  function injectWidget() {
    const s = document.createElement('script');
    s.async = true;
    s.src = 'https://widget.intercom.io/widget/' + appId;
    document.head.appendChild(s);
  }

  function initMessenger() {
    // Nothing to do while the widget's own DOM is still on the page. On a ClientRouter
    // swap it never is; the guard just keeps this idempotent if that ever changes.
    if (document.getElementById('intercom-frame')) return;

    // Intercom's stock call queue: buffers calls until widget.js replaces this stub and
    // boots from window.intercomSettings. Stock uses `arguments`; rest params replay
    // identically when widget.js applies each queued entry.
    const queue = function (...args) {
      queue.c(args);
    };
    queue.q = [];
    queue.c = function (args) {
      queue.q.push(args);
    };
    window.Intercom = queue;

    // First paint shouldn't compete with the widget; later navigations are already idle.
    if (document.readyState === 'complete') injectWidget();
    else window.addEventListener('load', injectWidget, { once: true });
  }

  initMessenger();
  document.addEventListener('astro:after-swap', initMessenger);
</script>
